# Line endings are LF, in the repository AND in the working tree, on every
# platform. `text=auto` alone only fixes the repository side and leaves checkout
# to core.autocrlf, which is true on the Windows dev machine: git then warns on
# every command that it will rewrite LF files as CRLF, and the working tree
# drifts into a mix of CRLF, LF and both-at-once files. `eol=lf` settles it, and
# it is the right default across this stack anyway, since everything runs on
# Linux inside Docker.
#
# What it costs when it is missing is not theoretical. A CRLF shebang fails
# outright and unhelpfully ("bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M"), and the stacks on
# the VPS are deployed by copy rather than by clone, so a Windows checkout that
# gained CRLF carries it straight to the server. In tmdb-front a config file
# deployed as CRLF against an LF copy produced a permanent diff, which someone
# silenced with git's `skip-worktree` bit, which in turn hid real drift in four
# other files and blocked deploys for months.
#
# Stack-wide convention, aligned on tmdb-crawler. If a Windows checkout shows
# files as modified right after this lands, the fix is `git add --renormalize .`,
# never turning the rules off.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit text files (LF everywhere, per the rule above)
*.py    text
*.sql   text
*.md    text
*.sh    text
*.txt   text
*.json  text
*.yml   text
*.yaml  text
*.cfg   text
*.ini   text
*.conf  text
*.env   text
Dockerfile text
.gitattributes text

# Binary files (never normalized)
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.db    binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.docx  binary
